// fx_hyp above 2^31 -- the case that used to be silently wrong.
//
// fx_hypfix quarters the radicand and hands it to fx_sqrt, whose exactness holds
// only below 2^31: above that fx_sqbit cannot start high enough and the root
// comes back far too small, with no fault raised. One quartering covered
// operands to 65535 and no further, which is what the header used to promise;
// fx_hypfix now recurses instead, so the whole word is covered.
//
// This is not a hypothetical range. A surface normal built from two edges of a
// thousand units has components in the millions, and the too-small magnitude made
// every Lambert term saturate -- a lit room rendered exactly as flat as an unlit
// one, with correct geometry and correct normals behind it. The values below are
// python3's math.isqrt of the exact sum of squares.
//
// The ceiling is the *result*, not the operands: fx_hyp answers an int, so a
// hypotenuse of 2^31 or more cannot come back however wide the radicand was. The
// largest case here is a fifth of a percent under that.

t_wide() {
  int fx_hyp_v = fx_hyp(2000000, 2000000);
  int fx_hyp_v2 = fx_hyp(710000, 0);
  t_wide2(fx_hyp_v, fx_hyp_v2);
} return void;
